const v11ReadA={id:"v11-read-a",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 69-72",title:"In Praise of Maintenance",description:"Ribbon-cuttings are good politics. A new bridge, a new market, or a new school building gives officials a photograph and residents something to point to. Fixing a leaking roof or clearing a drainage canal gives no one a photograph at all.\n\nThat difference shapes budgets. Across many local governments, money flows more easily to new projects than to the upkeep of old ones, even when the old ones are still badly needed. The result is a familiar sight: a health center opened with fanfare a decade ago, now with a broken water pump and a roof that leaks over the waiting area.\n\nNeglect is expensive. A small crack in a road surface, sealed early, costs little. Left alone through two rainy seasons, it lets water undermine the road beneath, and the repair becomes a reconstruction. Studies of roads and bridges in many countries have reached the same conclusion: regular maintenance costs far less over the life of a structure than repeated emergency repairs.\n\nI am not arguing against new projects. I am arguing that every new project should arrive with a maintenance budget attached, and that keeping what we already have in working order deserves as much credit as building something new.",alt:"Editorial arguing that budgets favor new projects over upkeep, that neglect is costlier than early repair, and that every new project should come with a maintenance budget."};
const v11ReadB={id:"v11-read-b",kind:"passage",label:"Babasahin para sa Aytem 73-76",title:"Ang Kakanin at ang Ulan",description:"Tuwing Sabado, nagtitinda si Mila ng puto at kutsinta sa tapat ng kapilya. Sampung taong gulang lamang siya, ngunit kabisado na niya ang presyo ng bawat isa at ang pangalan ng mga suki.\n\nIsang Sabado, malakas ang ulan. Kaunti ang taong lumabas, at pagsapit ng tanghali, kalahati pa ng bilao ang hindi nabebenta. Naisip ni Mila ang kanyang ina, na gumising nang alas-tres upang magluto.\n\nDumaan si Aling Cora, ang matandang nakatira sa dulo ng kalye. 'Magkano lahat?' tanong nito. Nagulat si Mila. 'Bakit po ninyo bibilhin lahat? Kayo lang po ang nakatira sa bahay ninyo.'\n\nNgumiti si Aling Cora. 'Hindi ko kakainin lahat. Ipamimigay ko sa mga batang naghihintay sa labas ng kapilya at sa mga trabahador sa kanto. Basa rin sila, at gutom.'\n\nPauwi, hawak ang walang lamang bilao, napansin ni Mila na hindi lamang siya ang natulungan ni Aling Cora nang araw na iyon. Kinabukasan, nagtabi siya ng dalawang kutsinta mula sa paninda at inihatid ito sa bahay sa dulo ng kalye.",alt:"Maikling kuwento tungkol sa batang nagtitinda ng kakanin sa tag-ulan, sa matandang bumili ng lahat upang ipamigay, at sa natutunan ng bata."};
const v11ReadC={id:"v11-read-c",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 77-80",title:"A Letter to a Former Student",description:"Dear Paolo,\n\nYour mother told me last week that you passed the licensure exam on your second attempt. I wanted to write before the congratulations from everyone else drowned out mine.\n\nI remember the day you failed the first time. You came to my house to return a book and told me, very calmly, that you were thinking of giving up and applying for something 'more realistic.' I said very little. I was afraid that anything I said would sound like a lecture, and you had heard enough of those.\n\nI want to tell you now what I did not say then. Failing that exam did not tell us how much you knew. It told us how you had prepared: alone, at night, after work, from notes you had copied from a friend. The second time, you joined a study group and asked for help with the parts you found hard. That was not luck. That was a better method.\n\nYou will meet harder tests than this one. When you do, I hope you remember that a bad result is information, not a verdict.\n\nWith pride and affection,\nMa'am Celia",alt:"Letter from a teacher congratulating a former student on passing a licensure exam and explaining that his first failure reflected his method, not his ability."};
